    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 041121\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n420 AM PDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Above-normal temperatures continue through Friday, with dangerous \n  heat returning to Death Valley.\n\n* A trough is set to bring cooler temperatures, increased winds\n  and low precipitation chances next weekend.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...through next Sunday.\n\nEnsemble guidance is in good agreement on anomalous ridging \nremaining in place through the upcoming work week. It looks like a \nweak disturbance will slide around the northern and eastern \nperiphery of the area on Monday-Tuesday, but this won't do much \nother then briefly knock temperatures down a few degrees. Highs are \nforecast to be 8-12 degrees above normal, with lows 5-10 degrees \nabove normal. This will be most pronounced in Death Valley where \nhighs are expected to be near 110 and overnight lows only getting \ndown into the mid 80s. Because of the resultant major to extreme \nheat risk, an Extreme Heat Warning has been issue for elevations \nbelow 2000 feet in Death Valley from 11am this morning through 8pm \nFriday.\n\nBy next weekend, we should begin to feel the effects of our next \nsystem. Still a ways out so uncertainty remains, but ensembles \nsuggest we could see a notable cooldown accompanied by gusty \nsouthwest winds. Precipitation potential will hinge on the \ninteraction with Hurricane Rachel in the east Pacific. Latest NBM \nhas low PoPs (15%) in the Sierra as early as Saturday, with a broad \nsmattering of 5-25% across the area on Sunday. These are likely to \nchange over the coming days as we get more clarity on the trough-\nhurricane interaction, but certainly something to monitor.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 12Z Forecast Package...\nExpect light winds generally following typical diurnal directional \npatterns through the TAF period. Light winds this morning under 5 kt \ncontinue for the next several hours. Around and after 17z, winds \nfavor a more E-NE direction but still remaining fairly light under 6-\n8 kt. There is a 20 percent chance of an occasional gust to 15 kts \nduring peak afternoon heating. Light S-SW winds develop after \nsunset, becoming variable at times during the overnight hours again \ntonight. No operationally significant cloud cover through the TAF \nperiod.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 12Z Forecast Package...Expect light and diurnal \nwinds for the Las Vegas Valley and CRV TAF sites with isolated gusts \n15-20 kt possible this afternoon and evening. At KBIH, light winds \nthis morning increase after 19z and become S-SE with gusts 20-25 kt \nthrough sunset. At KDAG, light and variable winds early this morning \nbecome easterly then increase above 10 kt after 16z...gusts 20-25 kt \nexpected during peak heating of the day diminishing after sunset. No \noperationally significant cloud cover through the TAF period.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Woods\nAVIATION...Moore\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
